Figure 3 illustrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response at the injection site, peaking around day 2 with a significant influx of Mac-2+ macrophages and Ly6C+ monocytes, alongside an early, sharp rise and fall of neutrophils. Confocal microscopy confirms that these recruited cells, particularly MHCII+, Ly6C+, and Mac-2+ cells, efficiently internalize Nano-11, whereas neutrophils do not. The inflammation largely resolves by day 21, indicating a self-limiting local reaction. This suggests Nano-11 effectively recruits immune cells to the injection site for antigen uptake and processing without causing prolonged inflammation.